            {
                "entry_id": "a4690250-671e-3bbb-9eae-bacb807ab6f5",
                "word": "bouif",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "95ce4c5f-0d8e-44e0-8635-2a9b27ef6425",
                        "entry_id": "a4690250-671e-3bbb-9eae-bacb807ab6f5",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "bouifs"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "7c9e2b8c-86c2-3e57-91d7-74cf4192be51",
                        "entry_id": "a4690250-671e-3bbb-9eae-bacb807ab6f5",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Savetier ; cordonnier."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "colloquial"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e4f799da-26cd-4831-94d4-28dbe88792f4",
                                "sense_id": "7c9e2b8c-86c2-3e57-91d7-74cf4192be51",
                                "text": "Il nous appelle fainéants ? un crampu qui ne saurait lever ses quilles du tabouret. Mais les jambes te remonteraient jusqu'à la gruette, pauvre bouif !"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "688b3983-a9cf-46f7-bd69-afa0842d52eb",
                                "sense_id": "7c9e2b8c-86c2-3e57-91d7-74cf4192be51",
                                "text": "Moi, je suis une artiste, mais lui, tout ce qu'il a récolté, c'est d'être un bouif chez qui on ne met les pieds qu'à la toute dernière extrémité de la semelle !"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "507fc39b-1868-4318-96b5-e9de42922f06",
                                "sense_id": "7c9e2b8c-86c2-3e57-91d7-74cf4192be51",
                                "text": "Pour les rares opérations plus délicates, nous avions tout près de chez nous un cordonnier professionnel, […]. J'appréciais sa conversation, parce que, comme tous les bouifs qui se respectent, il avait toujours la bouche pleine de clous, ce qui donnait à son discours du piquant, et même du suspense : je guettais toujours un accident qui ne vint jamais."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "f1a84a88-8237-4ed5-b775-fb1f6c4222be",
                        "source_entry_id": "a4690250-671e-3bbb-9eae-bacb807ab6f5",
                        "source_sense_id": "7c9e2b8c-86c2-3e57-91d7-74cf4192be51",
                        "target_lang_word": "cobbler",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "cobbler",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5fd44592-8ee0-3e52-81d5-3213666aa4fe",
                                        "entry_id": "1d152e5a-6640-34ef-8b60-f06f9581c9d2",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A person who repairs, and sometimes makes, shoes."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b8cde5f3-1aad-4ce4-9138-fde53b41bf1f",
                                                "sense_id": "5fd44592-8ee0-3e52-81d5-3213666aa4fe",
                                                "text": "This honest Cobler has done what he might: / That Statesmen in their Shoes might walk upright. / But rotten Shoes of Spannish running-leather: / No Coblers skill, can stitch them strong together."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "943022e9-ff7c-4dfa-86b7-b0aa3bdddf49",
                                                "sense_id": "5fd44592-8ee0-3e52-81d5-3213666aa4fe",
                                                "text": "[W]hat would they think of a French cobler cutting ſhoes for ſeveral of his fellow-ſubjects out of an old apple-tree?"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "db04008a-fca3-4709-bcbc-294c3ccac81f",
                                                "sense_id": "5fd44592-8ee0-3e52-81d5-3213666aa4fe",
                                                "text": "All honeſt jogg trotmen, who go on ſmoothly and dully, and write hiſtory and politics, and are praiſed; and who, had they been bred coblers, would all their lives have only mended ſhoes, but never made them."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a8faf881-fef1-4a4e-9705-34cec9420e5a",
                                                "sense_id": "5fd44592-8ee0-3e52-81d5-3213666aa4fe",
                                                "text": "But what of that vast number of the human kind who were always in the background? What of the hewers of wood and drawers of water, the swineherds, the shepherds, the carpenters, the hedgers and cobblers?"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8636ebf5-e135-3830-9455-cee5bba00b7f",
                                        "entry_id": "1d152e5a-6640-34ef-8b60-f06f9581c9d2",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A sheep left to the end to be sheared (for example, because its wool is filthy, or because it is difficult to catch)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Australia",
                                            "New-Zealand",
                                            "slang"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4be88a5e-b0cc-30fc-8cd0-afffa72c5a2c",
                                        "entry_id": "1d152e5a-6640-34ef-8b60-f06f9581c9d2",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A person who cobbles (“to assemble or mend in an improvised or rough way”); a clumsy workman."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ccd74f66-641f-40bb-87a0-390f943ee1a7",
                                                "sense_id": "4be88a5e-b0cc-30fc-8cd0-afffa72c5a2c",
                                                "text": "Truely Sir, in reſpect of a fine Workman, / I am but as you would ſay, a Cobler."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "cobbler",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "cd94cbe6-986b-33e6-94b9-312e7793a18c",
                                        "entry_id": "cf09f62e-3b79-38d1-9743-ee63cb12a032",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An (iced) alcoholic drink containing spirit or wine, with lemon juice and sugar."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "US"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bf6a51d0-94ee-4f31-bb87-9e6fa50fa9db",
                                                "sense_id": "cd94cbe6-986b-33e6-94b9-312e7793a18c",
                                                "text": "[H]e produced a very large tumbler, piled up to the brim with little blocks of clear transparent ice, through which one or two thin slices of lemon, and a golden liquid of delicious appearance, appeared from the still depths below, to the loving eye of the spectator. […] \"This wonderful invention, sir,\" said Mark, tenderly patting the empty glass, \"is called a cobbler. Sherry cobbler when you name it long; cobbler, when you name it short.[…]\""
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4dec9143-e613-4086-b701-6c8d8113b157",
                                                "sense_id": "cd94cbe6-986b-33e6-94b9-312e7793a18c",
                                                "text": "In the creed of Asirvadam the Brahmin, the drinker of strong drink is a Pariah, and the eater of cow's flesh is damned already. If, then, he can tell a cocktail from a cobbler, and scientifically discriminate between a julep and a gin-sling, it must be because the Vedas are unclasped to him; for in the Vedas all things are taught."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f4655b18-6e20-4883-9f99-615bb450b262",
                                                "sense_id": "cd94cbe6-986b-33e6-94b9-312e7793a18c",
                                                "text": "It was very hot when Captain Littledale was here; he did nothing but drink sherry cobblers."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "cobbler",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "eebb021f-9774-3f5c-9b4c-cf0585db2a56",
                                        "entry_id": "235d5348-ce0f-3a52-8507-1a51d2d23e6c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A roadworker who lays cobbles."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "cobbler",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a3946662-d1cc-3e2f-8abe-0cbf19f7283a",
                                        "entry_id": "167baae0-d4a0-36ec-9f75-631449f9bed3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The shiny, hard seed of the horse chestnut tree (Aesculus hippocastanum), especially when used in the game of the same name (sense 1.2); a conker, a horse chestnut."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "British",
                                            "dialectal"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "177f1955-cb6f-4474-830f-eb47b538241d",
                                                "sense_id": "a3946662-d1cc-3e2f-8abe-0cbf19f7283a",
                                                "text": "[\"A]n' 'e was mad, an' so he snatched my cobbler an' run off with it. An' so I run after 'im, an' when I was gettin' hold of him, 'e dodged, an' it ripped 'is collar. But I got my cobbler—\" He pulled from his pocket a black old horse-chestnut hanging on a string. This old cobbler had \"cobbled\"—hit and smashed—seventeen other cobblers on similar strings. So this boy was proud of his veteran."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "18d7de85-eca7-3857-afab-328a5e36a584",
                                        "entry_id": "167baae0-d4a0-36ec-9f75-631449f9bed3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Synonym of conkers (“a game for two players in which the participants each have a horse-chestnut (known as a cobbler (sense 1.1) or conker) suspended from a length of string, and take turns to strike their opponent's conker with their own with the object of destroying the opponent's conker before their own is destroyed”)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "British",
                                            "dialectal"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "cobbler",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "96e921d0-5174-314d-8a52-5827a92921f8",
                                        "entry_id": "b8e6fdd4-e012-3e82-95a4-5e5e0432ffae",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Used as a name for various animals.",
                                            "Also estuary cobbler:",
                                            "The South Australian catfish (Cnidoglanis macrocephalus), a species of catfish native to Australia which has dorsal and pectoral fins bearing sharp, venomous spines."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Australia"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f0364e33-a002-4b80-b2e2-f2da20bcf8c4",
                                                "sense_id": "96e921d0-5174-314d-8a52-5827a92921f8",
                                                "text": "Fished for cobblers in the evening. The warbler sings its night-song."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "65e9df0a-e25f-3dfa-b31e-86bf35d2564a",
                                        "entry_id": "b8e6fdd4-e012-3e82-95a4-5e5e0432ffae",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Used as a name for various animals.",
                                            "Also estuary cobbler:",
                                            "The soldier or South Australian cobbler (Gymnapistes marmoratus), a brown fish native to southern Australian estuaries which is not closely related to Cnidoglanis macrocephalus, but also has venemous spines on its dorsal and pectoral fins."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Australia"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "113f76f9-4537-355a-a54b-c13518732dc1",
                                        "entry_id": "b8e6fdd4-e012-3e82-95a4-5e5e0432ffae",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Used as a name for various animals.",
                                            "Also river cobbler: basa (Pangasius bocourti), an edible species of shark catfish native to the Chao Phraya and Mekong river basins in Southeast Asia."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "British"
                                        ],
